      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To solve the problem of finding the largest divisor of 342 that also divides 285, we need to find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of these two numbers. Here are the steps to find the solution:\n\n1. **Factorize 342 and 285:**\n\n   - **Factorize 342:**\n     \\[\n     342 \\div 2 = 171\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     171 \\div 3 = 57\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     57 \\div 3 = 19\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     19 \\div 19 = 1\n     \\]\n     So, the prime factorization of 342 is:\n     \\[\n     342 = 2 \\times 3^2 \\times 19\n     \\]\n\n   - **Factorize 285:**\n     \\[\n     285 \\div 3 = 95\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     95 \\div 5 = 19\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     19 \\div 19 = 1\n     \\]\n     So, the prime factorization of 285 is:\n     \\[\n     285 = 3 \\times 5 \\times 19\n     \\]\n\n2. **Identify the common factors:**\n\n   From the factorizations above, we see that both 342 and 285 have 19 in common.\n\n3. **Determine the greatest common divisor (GCD):**\n\n   The GCD is the product of the common prime factors with the lowest exponent. Here, the common factor is 19.\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{GCD}(342, 285) = 19\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the largest divisor of 342 that is also a factor of 285 is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{19}\n\\]",
